  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A warm front on Thursday will bring the next \nchance for widespread rain. \n\nBy Thursday, high pressure moves offshore with southerly return\nflow behind a warm front will bring dew points into the low \n70s. Showers will start moving into western areas before \ndaybreak and continue through the day. \n\nAs for the hazards, there is a marginal (1 out of 4) risk for \nexcessive rainfall. Precipitable water values increase to \nperhaps as high as 150% of normal for this time of year.\nGuidance suggests that the heaviest precipitation will be\ngenerally north and west of the I-95 corridor and much of the\nheavy recent rains have been further south. At this point the\nthreat for widespread flooding appears to be limited. \n\nFor severe hazards, will have to continue to monitor, but\ninstability and shear appear to be limited with this event,\ntherefore minimizing the severe potential. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A cold front will cross the area Friday.\n\nThe timing of this cold front will be critical in the risk for \nthunderstorm hazards. There are differences between models in \nboth the timing, and if this will be a single cold front, or an \ninitial cold front with a reinforcing shot of cold air advection\ncoming in behind it. Saturday and Sunday appear to be dry across\nmost of the area with the exception of southern areas Saturday\nwhere low probabilities of showers and storms will exist.",
